<%= render layout: "guides/frame", locals: { guide: @guide } do %>
  <p>
    &ldquo;T3&rdquo; meant three documents that travelled with every change of ownership: transaction
    information, transaction history and the transaction statement. One of those is gone.
  </p>

  <section>
    <h2>Transaction history ended in 2023</h2>
    <p>
      The statute ended the provision and receipt of transaction history on November 27, 2023, when the enhanced
      requirements took effect for most of the supply chain. Since then, the records that change hands are
      transaction information and the transaction statement.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Transaction information (TI)</h2>
    <p>
      TI describes what was transferred and between whom: the product&rsquo;s name, strength and dosage form,
      NDC, container size and number of containers, lot number, the transaction date (and the shipment date, if more
      than 24 hours later), and
      the business names and addresses of the seller and buyer. Under the enhanced requirements it also carries
      each package&rsquo;s product identifier, electronically.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Transaction statement (TS)</h2>
    <p>
      The TS is the seller&rsquo;s attestation that, among other things, it is authorized, received the product
      from an authorized trading partner, received the required records, and did not knowingly ship suspect or
      illegitimate product.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What a pharmacy must do with them</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Accept ownership of product only when you receive its TI and TS.</li>
      <li>Keep them for at least six years after the transaction.</li>
      <li>In a recall or an investigation of suspect or illegitimate product, produce the records you received
        within two business days of a request from FDA or another appropriate federal or state official.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Most pharmacies receive these through their wholesaler&rsquo;s portal. The useful test is how quickly you can
      pull them for one specific lot.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Small dispensers and electronic, package-level data</h2>
    <%= render "guides/qualify" %>
    <p>
      For qualifying small dispensers, FDA has deferred the enhanced requirements, including receiving and keeping
      package-level data electronically, until <%= DscsaDeadline.label %>. Ask each wholesaler now how they will
      send it, and whether your systems can receive and store it.
    </p>
  </section>
<% end %>
